PRESENT IN THIS SOURCE TREE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Files that are not this project's work, kept here because a test or a machine
cannot do without them. No code here is derived from any of them.

Spectrum and clone ROMs

    machine/core/src/main/resources/roms/*.rom

    Copyright Amstrad, Datel Microelectronics, Nine Tiles Networks Ltd, Garry
    Lancaster, Andrew Owen and Patrick Persson, each allowing distribution on
    their own terms, which are not the same terms. Which one covers which file
    is set out in README.copyright in that directory, and that file is part of
    this notice.

    A machine needs its ROM to boot, and the emulator ships them for that.

POKE listings

    machine/pokes/src/main/resources/pokes/<letter or digit>/*.pok

    Three thousand six hundred and eighty-three cheat listings for published
    games, in the .pok format Spectrum emulators read, named by the title,
    year and publisher of the game each is for. They are the work of the
    people who found the pokes, over decades; no file records who wrote it,
    none carries a licence, and this project is not their author.

    Taken from the AllTipshopPokes collection
    <https://github.com/ladyeklipse/all-tipshop-pokes>, made by Lady Eklipse,
    which declares no licence of its own and which gathered them by crawling
    the Tipshop <http://www.the-tipshop.co.uk/>, run by Gerard Sweeney, where
    the people who found the pokes published them. Bundled here so that the
    cheat dialog works with nothing to download.

    A poke is a description and an address to write a byte to. Nothing here
    is derived from them: they are read at run time and applied to whatever
    game is loaded.

RZX Archive catalogue

    machine/core/src/main/resources/rzx/rzx-archive.json

    Copyright the RZX Archive <https://www.rzxarchive.co.uk> and the people
    who submitted the recordings it lists, whose names it records.

    Four thousand two hundred and ninety-one entries describing recorded
    playthroughs: a title, a note, the submitter, the archive's own page, the
    Spectrum Computing id of the game, and where the file can be had. It is
    the catalogue and not the recordings - no RZX file is here - and the two
    hundred and forty-three entries the archive marks as distribution denied
    are recorded as such rather than dropped. Gathered from the archive's
    browse pages, one a second, because it offers no interface to ask.

    Read to answer whether a game the browser found has anyone's playthrough
    against it, by an id the two sites share.

Z80 instruction test vectors

    emulator/src/test/resources/fuse/tests.in
    emulator/src/test/resources/fuse/tests.expected
    emulator/src/test/resources/fuse/tests.names

    Copyright Philip Kendall and contributors. GNU General Public License,
    version 2 or later. Taken unmodified from the Fuse emulator, which
    distributes them.

    One thousand three hundred and fifty-six tests that say what a Z80 does
    to its registers, its memory and its clock, event by event. This project's
    processor is held to them; nothing here is a translation of them, and they
    reach no artefact but the test jar.

Microdrive cartridge

    machine/devices/interface1/src/test/resources/success.mdr

    Same origin and licence as the vectors above: a formatted cartridge of 179
    sectors from the Fuse distribution.

    Read by the two tests that ask whether the Interface 1's head gives back
    what a cartridge holds. That it is somebody else's file is the point: they
    are bytes this project did not write.
